[size=5][b]What it does[/b][/size]

Two halves, sharing one settings page.

[b]Flinches.[/b] A torso animation layered over whatever the character is already doing, on every instance of damage however small. It does not interrupt them - they keep walking, keep swinging.

[b]Staggers and deaths.[/b] Replaces the engine's own [font=Courier New]hit1[/font]-[font=Courier New]hit5[/font] and [font=Courier New]death*[/font] animations with endorphin-simulated falls, cut by hand and chosen to match the direction of the blow: where the attacker stands, which way the weapon swings, and how hard it landed. [font=Courier New]knockdown[/font] and [font=Courier New]knockout[/font] are left alone.

Applies to the player, to every NPC, and to creatures that use the humanoid animation set. The stagger half is third person only.

[size=5][b]Installation[/b][/size]

[list]
[*]Install the dependency [url=https://www.nexusmods.com/morrowind/mods/60256]Max Yari's Script Services (MSS)[/url]. Most of my Lua mods need it now. It should come [b]before[/b] all my mods in the load order - putting it at the very top of the list does no harm.
[*]Install this mod [b]with a mod organiser[/b]: download the archive and drag it into the organiser of your choice - [url=https://github.com/ModOrganizer2/modorganizer/releases]Mod Organizer 2[/url] on Windows, [url=https://github.com/grazelandsnomad/nerevarine_organizer/releases/tag/v0.70]Nerevarine Organizer[/url] on Linux. [b]Or[/b] [url=https://modding-openmw.com/tips/installing-mods/]read this tutorial[/url] on installing mods with the launcher or by hand - it is also very easy.
[*]Enable the mod's [font=Courier New].omwscripts[/font] file in the [b]Content Files[/b] tab of the OpenMW launcher.
[/list]

Nothing else is required. LuaPhysics is not used - the gear-off-a-corpse feature that needed it now lives in The Combat Juice.

Developed and tested against OpenMW 0.51.

[size=5][b]What is in the pool[/b][/size]

33 clips, simulated in endorphin and retargeted onto the Morrowind skeleton. Each is cut for one job, and the two jobs do not share clips: a stagger ends with the character still on their feet, a fall ends on the floor.

Directions are from the [b]viewer's[/b] side, the way the clips are named - a player looking at the character they hit.

[list]
[*][b]11 staggers[/b] - 2 back, 4 to the left (one of them a long one), 5 to the right (one long). Nothing forward yet.
[*][b]22 falls[/b] - 3 forward (2 long), 8 back (2 long), 6 left (1 long), 5 right (1 long).
[/list]

Four of the staggers are mirrors of others, which is how both sides ended up with the same repertoire including a long fall. Nothing travels forward yet.

